                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "66eb2786-5840-4193-aada-314574a27981",
                        "source_entry_id": "b069399a-00f7-3570-a178-821476fa9199",
                        "source_sense_id": "18cb3934-24b2-3816-9bed-6fec5e97a145",
                        "target_lang_word": "sentiment",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "sentiment",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e34ef92e-3f1c-3e00-8710-5dc276b00cbf",
                                        "entry_id": "20e38e9b-ca20-35a6-8c10-10407753449d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A general thought, feeling, or sense."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "08004555-d2c9-49c1-b793-9bbb96cb74fe",
                                                "sense_id": "e34ef92e-3f1c-3e00-8710-5dc276b00cbf",
                                                "text": "The sentiment emerged that we were acting too soon."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "49af6ee6-ca3a-4c0b-8702-e30d6e145cdd",
                                                "sense_id": "e34ef92e-3f1c-3e00-8710-5dc276b00cbf",
                                                "text": "\"Now, my dear young friend,\" continued the bookseller, \"you seem fond of reason; let me talk a little reason to you. Here, take your pamphlet again: there is good material in it, but it requires the making up. Leave out some of your arguments, and throw in a few sentiments,—something about free-born Britons and wooden shoes! Englishmen like to have a few sentiments ready for after-dinner use, in case of a speech..."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4759498c-e357-4217-8105-19f2e3bc2196",
                                                "sense_id": "e34ef92e-3f1c-3e00-8710-5dc276b00cbf",
                                                "text": "The departure was not unduly prolonged.[…]Within the door Mrs. Spoker hastily imparted to Mrs. Love a few final sentiments on the subject of Divine Intention in the disposition of buckets; farewells and last commiserations; a deep, guttural instigation to the horse; and the wheels of the waggonette crunched heavily away into obscurity."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0912ecc3-36b7-35da-a47a-9b8dd91b2646",
                                        "entry_id": "20e38e9b-ca20-35a6-8c10-10407753449d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Feelings, especially tender feelings, as apart from reason or judgment, or of a weak or foolish kind."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a383be8c-64da-45cc-9380-2ac2d2463212",
                                                "sense_id": "0912ecc3-36b7-35da-a47a-9b8dd91b2646",
                                                "text": "Near-synonyms: feels; maudlinness"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "260d2036-6894-418c-9f25-022f79849df0",
                                                "sense_id": "0912ecc3-36b7-35da-a47a-9b8dd91b2646",
                                                "text": "Good decision-making is not governed by mere sentiment."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c33c961d-62f1-4fd7-b3b6-253902439bb1",
                                                "sense_id": "0912ecc3-36b7-35da-a47a-9b8dd91b2646",
                                                "text": "To do the job thoroughly sentiment must be ignored and it seems inevitable that the famous Great Hall and the Doric Arch will have to be sacrificed to progress."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "20160036-e378-4a1d-b984-5621238662c4",
                                                "sense_id": "0912ecc3-36b7-35da-a47a-9b8dd91b2646",
                                                "text": "Despite personal schisms and differences in spiritual experience, there is a very coherent theology of Snape shared between the wives. To examine this manifestation of religious fandom, I will first discuss the canon scepticism and anti-Rowling sentiment that helps to contextualise the wider belief in Snape as a character who extends beyond book and film."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b225edee-4bf6-43c2-b95e-9f064fc3c879",
                        "source_entry_id": "b069399a-00f7-3570-a178-821476fa9199",
                        "source_sense_id": "18cb3934-24b2-3816-9bed-6fec5e97a145",
                        "target_lang_word": "affection",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "affection",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7173891e-385b-3f24-8bb7-c819c812bc0b",
                                        "entry_id": "8af1ccf5-14ad-34c6-8c79-db638249d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The act of affecting or acting upon."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "731106d6-e716-34f9-b582-27e0df530fc8",
                                        "entry_id": "8af1ccf5-14ad-34c6-8c79-db638249d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The state of being affected, especially: a change in, or alteration of, the emotional state of a person or other animal, caused by a subjective affect (a subjective feeling or emotion), which arises in response to a stimulus which may result from either thought or perception."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "23283372-0df1-31bf-93fb-354e0f9b5ad2",
                                        "entry_id": "8af1ccf5-14ad-34c6-8c79-db638249d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An attribute; a quality or property; a condition."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "094c0f8b-c0ec-4005-9443-11344823470e",
                                                "sense_id": "23283372-0df1-31bf-93fb-354e0f9b5ad2",
                                                "text": "A Porism is a proposition in which it is proposed to demonstrate that some one thing, or more things than one, are given, to which, as also to each of innumerable other things, not given indeed, but which have the same relation to those which are given, it is to be shewn that there belongs some common affection described in the proposition."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "88db41c7-22a5-3ef2-9e31-1b722d27a458",
                                        "entry_id": "8af1ccf5-14ad-34c6-8c79-db638249d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An emotion; a feeling or natural impulse acting upon and swaying the mind."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "743bd066-64ad-4cb3-a562-9755d621f981",
                                                "sense_id": "88db41c7-22a5-3ef2-9e31-1b722d27a458",
                                                "text": "Our affections for wild animals are distributed very unevenly. Take insects."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "afcb332f-ff1f-4d75-85d2-00caf288a3b4",
                                                "sense_id": "88db41c7-22a5-3ef2-9e31-1b722d27a458",
                                                "text": "It is known that each individual has a variety of affections, one affection when in joy, another when in grief, another when in sympathy and compassion, another when in sincerity and truth, another when in love and charity, another when in zeal or in anger, another when in simulation and deceit, another when in quest of honor and glory, and so on."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                        "entry_id": "8af1ccf5-14ad-34c6-8c79-db638249d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A feeling of love or strong attachment."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8a08810f-a2b9-489a-8c97-4a6ebd86e674",
                                                "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                                "text": "I have a lot of affection for my little sister."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "09e8b2bc-7b27-41ac-83bd-2fea9ebe7aab",
                                                "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                                "text": "The marriage therapist suggested they show each other more affection."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f9fef831-3023-4cd5-9986-f1a6e23cd8f6",
                                                "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                                "text": "Mr. Bennet missed his second daughter exceedingly; his affection for her drew him oftener from home than anything else could do. He delighted in going to Pemberley, especially when he was least expected."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "78a60b5c-c12e-4e56-a09a-4bc3de6fda9d",
                                                "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                                "text": "What is more, they are protected from even such discomfort as the dislike of his prisoners may cause to a gaoler by the hypnotism of the convention that the natural relation between husband and wife and parent and child is one of intense affection, and that to feel any other sentiment towards a member of one's family is to be a monster."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2bf64a08-5c95-420f-80ea-42da39f87a64",
                                                "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                                "text": "\"Did you ever like me back, Ryan? All those years, I didn't know how to show my affection for you, so I wasn't sure if you weren't getting it or you just didn't care. But I need to know which it was.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c4795bc4-5d66-4eb5-880a-b06eebefcb14",
                                                "sense_id": "4ab04238-0626-3367-bc54-83151b609e68",
                                                "text": "Dated a girl that I hate, for the attention / She only made it two days, what a connection / It's like you'd do anything, for my affection / You're going all about it in the worst ways"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e38e657d-7419-339e-b640-65c1d5f9c49d",
                                        "entry_id": "8af1ccf5-14ad-34c6-8c79-db638249d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A disease; a morbid symptom; a malady."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic",
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "69e13e11-d928-44d2-97a2-5fad30fb0e80",
                                                "sense_id": "e38e657d-7419-339e-b640-65c1d5f9c49d",
                                                "text": "a pulmonary affection"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4404baa6-9f42-45e4-b339-5b6040468108",
                                                "sense_id": "e38e657d-7419-339e-b640-65c1d5f9c49d",
                                                "text": "The recedent or retrograde form is marked by a sudden subsidence of the inflammatory state of the joints, succeeded immediately by an affection of some internal part, where is thenceforth the seat of the morbid manifestations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2b5cfe34-39dc-4389-a140-41b81161d4de",
                                                "sense_id": "e38e657d-7419-339e-b640-65c1d5f9c49d",
                                                "text": "A heavy clay soil is bad for all neuralgics, and the house should be dry, and on a sandy or gravel soil. The desideratum for all neuralgic affections is perpetual summer […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "affection",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cdf12a52-7e63-3270-b6c4-1fb53a40a3a3",
                                        "entry_id": "00758d05-2e03-3429-8175-e539f560093d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To feel affection for."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d7884f88-c359-4a09-b88e-766bc9df11ff",
                                                "sense_id": "cdf12a52-7e63-3270-b6c4-1fb53a40a3a3",
                                                "text": "Why, truth is truth, I do not think my lady Isabella ever much affectioned my young lord, your son: yet he was a sweet youth as one should see."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
